WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:04.130
I want to talk to you today
about using Deadline along

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:06.100
with the Google Cloud platform.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.561
I'll start off with an
overview of Deadline.

00:00:09.561 --> 00:00:11.530
It was developed to
allow render farm

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:14.570
to be shared between
applications, departments,

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.310
and of course, between
facilities as well.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.960
It's been used by large
and small companies

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.530
alike around the world.

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:23.360
It does not use a
single manager design

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.220
it's meant to be distributed.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:28.720
Instead the slave logic
that is built into the line

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.640
picks up the work
based on the set

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.370
of rules, the criteria,
and scheduling logic

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:34.580
that you provide.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.630
Right now deadlines supports or
70 applications out of the box,

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:41.830
and can be added via Python
and the native applications

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:44.136
scripting languages.

00:00:44.136 --> 00:00:45.760
Deadline was meant
to be artist driven.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.290
We want it to be really
easy, one click to install.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:54.880
We want it to be able to render,
simulate, execute commands, do

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:55.550
software builds.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.200
We actually use
internally at Thinkbox.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.594
Do out own software builds.

00:00:59.594 --> 00:01:01.885
And of course we have extensive
public and private file

00:01:01.885 --> 00:01:04.709
support, which I'm here
to talk about today.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:07.000
We want you to have the
best of both worlds,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.590
use your existing
infrastructure,

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:11.120
your existing Deadline's
set up, your local hardware,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:12.650
plus use on demand.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.500
And the best way to do that of
course, Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.820
We have extensive support
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.430
The way we do that is with
the new module in Deadline,

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:25.310
called the virtual
machine extension.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:29.404
It's able to launch and
terminate virtual machines.

00:01:29.404 --> 00:01:30.820
We can do it through
a cloud panel

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.195
or we can do in an automated
way through a program called

00:01:33.195 --> 00:01:36.250
Bouncer, I'll tell you a
little bit about that as we go.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.140
And we have a sort of
fancy Cloud installer

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.396
if you want to get
going on the Cloud

00:01:41.396 --> 00:01:43.020
very quickly with
the Cloud repository,

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.780
you can use our Cloud
wizard to do that.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.640
These are the sort of
common Deadline components.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.650
I'm going to spend
a lot of time here,

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.940
but the short version
is the repository

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.350
in the database form the queue.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:59.030
And the key worker
applications is

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.360
Slave, that's responsible
for controlling

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.990
my max nuke whatever, doing the
actual rendering on the render

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:06.750
node.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.330
There's various
support applications.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:12.620
Pulse monitors your
interface into the queue,

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.110
Draft for lightweight
compositing,

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:17.896
and of course you
licensed server.

00:02:17.896 --> 00:02:19.770
Let's talk about the
Cloud-related components

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:20.540
of Deadline.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.340
Deadline Balancer
is the mechanism

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.240
that we have for doing
automated launching

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:27.849
and terminating
virtual machines.

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:29.890
I'll talk about that in
a little bit more detail.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:32.250
We have an Asset Crawler
script essentially

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:36.950
that sits on each Cloud region
that you enable and watches

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:39.310
for assets and makes sure
that jobs don't try to render

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:40.951
before the assets are ready.

00:02:40.951 --> 00:02:42.450
On the Google side,
of course you're

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.980
going to a Google Cloud account.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.180
That's your own personal
account or company account,

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:47.830
depending on how
you're using it.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:51.110
A virtual network
within the Cloud,

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.530
this is sort of a little bubble
within your Cloud account

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:56.660
where you build a network
to do the rendering.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.400
Virtual private network
forms the bridge

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.290
between your local facility
and your Cloud account

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.590
for secure communications.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:04.920
Storage on the Cloud.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.200
And then you'll also need the
virtual machines themselves

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.410
that have the information
for the operating

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:14.180
system, the worker application
like my Deadline slate

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:15.510
and so forth.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:16.890
Those are the
components and I'll

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:21.810
start to talk a little bit
about how it all fits together.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:25.110
We sort of promote a
hybridized approach.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:28.300
We call it Pop-up Cloud as
our sort of buzzword for that.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.705
We combine local compute
resources will off

00:03:30.705 --> 00:03:33.520
site private or public
Cloud resources.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.910
You have a local office and
the VPN that connects you up

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:38.360
to your Cloud.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:41.830
This is enabled through
our Cloud provider dialogue

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:42.830
within Deadline.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.830
This is where you can add Cloud
regions from your provider

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:47.850
and configure all
the credentials

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.105
and so forth so that
once it's configured

00:03:50.105 --> 00:03:54.840
Deadline can talk to the
Cloud and get things done.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.037
Let me tell you about
some of the ways

00:03:57.037 --> 00:03:58.370
that Deadline can be configured.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:03:59.870
It's meant to be
extremely flexible.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:02.880
And so sometimes people ask
me, how should we set this up?

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.480
It really depends
on your use case,

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:06.410
there's a lot of different
ways you can do it.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:07.826
The various
components of Deadline

00:04:07.826 --> 00:04:10.844
can be installed in different
locations, it's very tolerant.

00:04:10.844 --> 00:04:12.760
As long as the pieces
could talk to each other

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:14.840
they can pretty
much exist anywhere.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:18.720
In this particular configuration
for direct submission

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.604
the database and repository
are up in the Cloud.

00:04:21.604 --> 00:04:23.770
You've got the balancer
application up in the Cloud,

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:25.603
it's starting and
stopping virtual machines.

00:04:25.603 --> 00:04:26.890
You've got some Cloud storage.

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:28.848
On your local side you
probably have an artist,

00:04:28.848 --> 00:04:31.710
you might have a couple of
slaves running locally as well,

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.820
it'd be driven by that
Cloud based repository,

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.210
and got your local file store.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.490
One possible configuration.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:39.190
Another possible configuration.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:42.124
Move the database and
repository locally.

00:04:42.124 --> 00:04:43.540
Essentially the
same, the balancer

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.050
might be local in
that case as well,

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:47.270
or it could be, again,
up in the Cloud.

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:49.676
But still you're talking
to a set of cloud slaves

00:04:49.676 --> 00:04:51.050
and a set of local
slaves, you've

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:53.140
got your local storage,
your cloud storage.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:56.600
And you have that flexibility
again of your local system

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.270
and your Cloud system.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.750
And then a third
configuration could be

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:03.340
that you have two repositories.

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:07.700
Some of the companies that we
work with really like to you

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.874
might say micromanage
their Cloud rendering.

00:05:09.874 --> 00:05:11.290
And so what they
do is they set up

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:14.920
an entirely separate repository,
basically a separate render

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:17.560
farm, in the Cloud, they got
their own local render farm.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:19.143
And a lot of times
this happens simply

00:05:19.143 --> 00:05:21.900
because they have a local farm
that's all working perfectly,

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:22.970
they want to play
with this stuff,

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:24.340
they want to do it
carefully, so they'll set up

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:25.574
a separate farm in the Cloud.

00:05:25.574 --> 00:05:26.990
Once they get their
head around it

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:29.130
they'll probably move back to
one of the other configurations

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:31.020
where there's only
a single repository.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.971
But again, it's quite seamless.

00:05:32.971 --> 00:05:35.054
You can use whichever
configuration works for you.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:41.481
The mechanism that we use
to decide whether a job can

00:05:41.481 --> 00:05:43.480
go to the Cloud or not
is called Group Mappings,

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:44.760
this is the interface for it.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.440
Let me talk more conceptually
about how it works.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:49.237
Deadline has a notion of groups.

00:05:49.237 --> 00:05:50.820
Whenever you submit
a job to Deadline,

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:51.910
you submit it to a group.

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:54.197
The group can be sort
of anything you define.

00:05:54.197 --> 00:05:55.780
Quite often you'll
see a configuration

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:58.722
like 3D simulation, I'm
looking at the column sort

00:05:58.722 --> 00:06:00.430
of in the middle that
says Group Mapping,

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.096
you have a 3D group,
a simulation group,

00:06:02.096 --> 00:06:03.370
a proxy group, a composite.

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:05.872
This could be whatever you want.

00:06:05.872 --> 00:06:07.630
On the left side is your queue.

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:10.070
This is sort of a schematic
representation of a queue,

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:12.660
there might be a Maya
job in there, a draft job

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:14.060
for doing proxy and Bifrost.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:17.030
Each of these jobs
has a group that

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:19.770
is associated with the
job when it's submitted.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:23.740
So what the Group Mapping does,
is it says, OK, for this group,

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.670
let's look at for example
3D at the top there,

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:30.090
I want to map that group to an
N1 standard for instance, four

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:33.870
core instance, and I want to
map it into a virtual machine

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:36.010
image that has Deadline
Slave installed,

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:37.700
it's got Maya it's got Max.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:40.050
Basically all the three
software, plus Deadlines

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.110
control software.

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:45.010
And what will happen then, is
that basically tells Deadline

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.067
that the 3D group is
enabled within this region,

00:06:47.067 --> 00:06:48.650
and these settings
are on a per region

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:50.524
basis, so if you have
multiple regions set up

00:06:50.524 --> 00:06:53.405
you can actually control
which sort of hardware

00:06:53.405 --> 00:06:55.975
and which VM images
that you use.

00:06:55.975 --> 00:06:57.850
At the bottom you'll
notice that, for example

00:06:57.850 --> 00:06:59.700
the composite group
doesn't have a mapping,

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:02.232
and all this is saying is that
for this particular region

00:07:02.232 --> 00:07:03.940
we don't want the job
going to the Cloud,

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:05.310
we'll just keep those locally.

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:08.430
So you have the-- can have a
job go to multiple regions, one

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.430
region, it all just depends
on the group mapping

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:12.471
so that you have a lot of
flexibility and control

00:07:12.471 --> 00:07:14.090
of that.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:16.850
Once your group
mappings are defined

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:21.200
and Deadline knows the job
is eligible to be processed

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:24.150
on the Cloud, the
program that will do that

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:25.120
is called Balancer.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:27.840
And Balancer basically
watches over the queue

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:31.870
and it says OK I see some
jobs and I see some mappings,

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:33.530
and so based on the
demand and based

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:36.830
on all of your configuration
settings and your budget,

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:39.050
I'm going to start up
some instances over here

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:41.500
or terminate some instances
over there and so forth.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:44.600
So, launching and terminating
virtual machine instances

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:45.740
by hand would be tedious.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:47.230
You could manually
launch them but it's not

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:48.280
going to be
efficient, so this is

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:49.590
an automated way of doing that.

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:53.050
Saves costs with regard
to starting and stopping

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:53.940
virtual machines.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:57.060
And it will balance
across regions.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:01.610
And the algorithm
that decides which

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:04.550
virtual machines get started and
when and all that is pluggable.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:05.970
So we provide a
default algorithm

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:07.670
that we feel pretty
efficient, but we

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:09.340
realize that studios
may want to hook

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:12.510
into other things you may want
to provide sort of deep status

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:13.160
controls.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.409
So the algorithm's pluggable,
you can look at the code

00:08:15.409 --> 00:08:18.170
that we provide and modify
it, copy it, make your own.

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:21.890
Come talk to us if you need
help, we're happy to help you.

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:25.210
Deadline, this is the
settings for Balancer

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:26.162
for a given region.

00:08:26.162 --> 00:08:27.620
One of the key
things look at there

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:29.870
is that green box in the
middle, that's the budget.

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:31.460
What the budget does
is it makes sure

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:35.900
that you don't exceed a certain
spend rate within that region.

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:40.260
That could be dollars per hour
or whatever units you want,

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:42.907
typically is dollars per hour.

00:08:42.907 --> 00:08:45.240
And the reason for that is
that because the algorithm is

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:49.626
pluggable, it's sometimes
possible to make a mistake,

00:08:49.626 --> 00:08:51.250
as what happens with
any computer code.

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:53.720
So the Balancer will
always make sure

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:55.817
that, no matter what
the algorithm says

00:08:55.817 --> 00:08:57.400
in terms of starting
virtual machines,

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:01.122
it may say start 500
million virtual machines,

00:09:01.122 --> 00:09:03.580
the Balancer will say what your
budget just doesn't support

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:05.767
that, so we're going to
clamp it your actual request

00:09:05.767 --> 00:09:06.850
to fit within your budget.

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:09.560
So sort of a safety mechanism.

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:12.950
And it also let's you
control your overall spend

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:15.440
for project as well.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:18.570
Deadline has, in
its current form,

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:21.290
something we call
asset awareness.

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:24.390
At present we don't do
automated asset management,

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:26.470
but we have a lot
of controls in there

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:28.450
to make sure that things
don't actually render

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:29.770
until your assets are ready.

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:33.320
So, for example,
the jobs haven't

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:36.110
required asset property,
and when that's populated

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:39.300
it says which assets are
required to process a job.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:41.270
The asset crawler
lives on the Cloud,

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:44.930
and it keeps an eye on which
assets are up on the Cloud.

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:47.480
And the Balancer then we'll
talk to that Asset Crawler

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:50.200
and say, hey, do you have
the assets for this job?

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:54.180
And if the Asset
Crawler says yes,

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:58.350
then the Balancer will green
light that job for the Cloud.

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:02.720
If it doesn't then Balancer
will just discount that job.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:10.110
And then finally the job will
be green lit for the Cloud,

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:14.110
but again it won't pick up
unless those assets are ready.

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:18.430
So this prevents errors,
prevents waste and spend.

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:21.762
So this is all fine and good.

00:10:21.762 --> 00:10:24.220
Is great that you can configure
deadline in all these ways,

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:25.636
you can do all
this stuff with it.

00:10:25.636 --> 00:10:28.260
But there's a little bit
of learning curve there.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:31.240
And so we want to help you
get started and with this as

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:33.740
quickly as possible so that you
can get your head around it.

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:36.340
We do that was something called
the Deadline Cloud Wizard.

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:39.895
What it does is it does an
automated set up of the pieces

00:10:39.895 --> 00:10:42.020
and you need to get a Cloud
Based repository render

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:42.990
farm going.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:45.790
Installs VPN, configures
Cloud storage,

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:49.565
installs temporary Deadline
and Balancer licensing,

00:10:49.565 --> 00:10:52.500
int installs a fully functional
repository and database,

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.370
it downloads example VM
images preconfigured with,

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:56.896
for example Maya
and Blender things

00:10:56.896 --> 00:10:58.270
like that so you
can see it work.

00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:00.350
It pre-populates the queue
with some example jobs,

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:02.266
you can basically see
the whole thing work out

00:11:02.266 --> 00:11:04.510
of your own account
very quickly.

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:08.379
So I have a video this is a
sort of a edited down video

00:11:08.379 --> 00:11:09.920
of actually running
the Cloud Wizard.

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:12.600
So you download the
Cloud Wizard from us,

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:15.640
install it on your
machine and fire it up.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.700
We're going to set up a Cloud
based farm right in front you.

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:20.690
There's a few prerequisites
you need to do.

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:23.140
Obviously you need to set up
your Google Cloud account,

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:26.725
you need to create a project,
and you enable the Google

00:11:26.725 --> 00:11:27.350
Compute Engine.

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:29.470
These things just
take a few minutes.

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:33.430
So hear are the
project IDs going in.

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:35.390
I'm getting the OAuth
credentials, which

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:40.170
allows the Cloud Wizard to
talk to your Cloud account,

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:44.530
to talk to Google, and take
action to install things,

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:45.090
to do things.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:49.482
So this is basically setting
up the authentication.

00:11:49.482 --> 00:11:52.420
And it is good.

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:53.370
So there.

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:54.510
Authentication completed.

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:57.120
The next thing it's going to
do is set up some SSH keys.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:59.440
It can be done
automatically for you.

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:02.210
I mentioned that you need
a little mini network

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:03.910
inside of your Cloud
account, so it's

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:07.160
going to set that up for you.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:08.980
Retrieving temporary licensing.

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:11.309
It's going to copy
the virtual machine

00:12:11.309 --> 00:12:12.850
images for
infrastructure, that means

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:14.930
the repository, your
database, your licensing,

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:17.860
as well as the demo instances.

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:20.400
It's setting up a
VPN firewall rules,

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:22.300
it's launching the
infrastructural VN

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:25.530
so that your repository and
database are coming online.

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:28.530
And It's telling
you to shut down

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:30.600
any VPN you might already
have going because it's

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:32.200
going to set up it's own.

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:34.280
Go and do that right now.

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:37.750
And once that's up and
running then pretty much

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:40.442
you have a secure connection
between your local workstation

00:12:40.442 --> 00:12:41.400
and your Cloud account.

00:12:44.208 --> 00:12:46.986
Finish up in a moment,
and license server set up

00:12:46.986 --> 00:12:48.610
is done now that it
has the Mac address

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:50.205
and has installed the licensing.

00:12:50.205 --> 00:12:52.038
It's now doing the last
minute configuration

00:12:52.038 --> 00:12:53.700
on the repository.

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:56.625
You can imagine doing this
by hand would take a while,

00:12:56.625 --> 00:12:58.500
so the whole idea of
the direction that we're

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:02.450
going with all of this is to
make it happen automatically,

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:05.220
quickly for you, basically
again pop-up Cloud.

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:08.290
So, here the Balancer
application is launched.

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:10.290
This is a brand new
repository so it's creating

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:13.340
an account for me on Deadline.

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:16.980
Here is Monitor, with a couple
of jobs in the upper left hand

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:17.480
corner.

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:19.240
I'm going to switch
into super user mode,

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:23.767
and go back to configure
Cloud providers.

00:13:23.767 --> 00:13:25.600
Here we can see the
Google Cloud region that

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:27.270
was set up automatically
by the Cloud Wizard

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:28.310
all the credentials
have been entered

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:30.320
based on what you put
into the Cloud Wizard

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:32.028
so you don't have to
go through all that.

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:39.340
Lots of settings and saves
time, Balancer options,

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:43.020
initial budget set to two,
just to keep it nice and safe

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:44.210
for testing and play.

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:46.455
These are the group mappings,
I talked about earlier.

00:13:46.455 --> 00:13:48.330
If we look at, for
example, the Blender group

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:50.410
we can see that it's been
set to n1-standard-1,

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:52.530
one core instance type,
and it's using the image

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:54.310
that has Blender installed.

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:57.920
So when a Blender
job is ready to go,

00:13:57.920 --> 00:13:59.880
which I'm about to do
right here by resuming

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:02.960
both of these jobs,
both Maya and Blender,

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:04.840
it will start those up.

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:07.830
Now one of those jobs, I didn't
actually-- you can't really

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:11.100
see the priority of the
jobs here, but one of them

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:14.270
has a little bit more
priority than the other.

00:14:14.270 --> 00:14:15.840
Balancer will
recognize that and try

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:19.120
to populate virtual
machines for that job first.

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:21.952
And we can see it
populating the state.

00:14:21.952 --> 00:14:23.410
It's running through
the algorithm,

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:25.770
the pluggable algorithm,
which tells it,

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:29.457
hey, we need a couple of
virtual machines fired up

00:14:29.457 --> 00:14:32.040
for Maya because that's what our
budget said that we could do.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:35.690
And then it starts firing
up virtual machines.

00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:38.300
And if I wanted to
go back and say well,

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:43.870
two isn't really enough
let's bump it up to 10.

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:45.944
Get things done a
little bit faster.

00:14:45.944 --> 00:14:47.610
And trust me the
numbers can get bigger,

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:49.290
I'll talk about that later.

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:52.490
And within a few moments, within
the next cycle of the bouncer,

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:54.859
again this is a
bit time compressed

00:14:54.859 --> 00:14:57.400
because the amount of time that
we have for the presentation.

00:14:57.400 --> 00:14:58.997
But it will
recognize that change

00:14:58.997 --> 00:15:00.580
and say oh you've
got more budget now,

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:02.920
so let's fire up
some additional VMs.

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:05.090
As the Maya job
completes, it will then

00:15:05.090 --> 00:15:07.540
go down to the job that
has next highest priority,

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:09.680
will start shutting down
the Maya virtual machines

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:11.872
and spinning up the
Blender virtual machines

00:15:11.872 --> 00:15:13.080
because that's what's needed.

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:15.460
It will also recognize, if
you have two different job

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:18.810
types that are using the exact
same virtual machine images,

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:20.810
it will just switch over
to those intelligently.

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:23.910
It wont shut things down
start them back up again.

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:26.260
So there's a lot of choice
of configuration here, we've

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:28.760
tried to keep it as flexible
as possible, and yet as

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:29.560
easy as possible.

00:15:29.560 --> 00:15:32.760
So now the Maya job's
done, firing up Blender,

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:36.955
we're seeing virtual
machines fire up for that

00:15:36.955 --> 00:15:37.830
through the Balancer.

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:42.090
So that's pretty much
it for the Cloud Wizard,

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:44.570
in terms of watching
demo of it run,

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:47.230
I think I'm just going to
skip forward at this point.

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:50.570
It's going to essentially
finish that job

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:55.070
and then terminate
those virtual machines.

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:58.320
So, quick recap on
the Cloud Wizard.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:00.010
It only usually takes
about 30 minutes

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:01.470
to run in most of our testing.

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:03.200
There's a little
bit of prerequisite,

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:05.241
like I said, that stuff
shouldn't really take you

00:16:05.241 --> 00:16:07.500
more than 15 to 30 minutes.

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:09.257
So I'd say in under
an hour you can

00:16:09.257 --> 00:16:11.090
be playing with the
Cloud render farm that's

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:13.380
running in your own account.

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:15.620
Thinkbox helps you out
with a 30 day evaluation,

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:19.370
so there's no cost on the
Thinkbox side to try this out.

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:20.946
Google's kind enough
to help you out

00:16:20.946 --> 00:16:24.502
with a $500 credit for
your Cloud compute time.

00:16:24.502 --> 00:16:25.960
That means essentially
that you can

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:29.430
get started with this
at no cost to you,

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:31.010
the only real cost is your time.

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:33.195
So figure couple
hours for that and you

00:16:33.195 --> 00:16:35.820
can actually play with this, get
your head around the concepts,

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:40.230
and think about how you might
want to do your implementation.

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:41.720
Does it scale?

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:44.000
You can see the number
on the left the middle?

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:44.730
No?

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:45.710
Make a bigger.

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:47.850
5,000 instances we've
tested for sure,

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:51.470
we didn't come close to
tapping out what system can do.

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:55.250
I'm quite certain that we could
reach many, many multiples

00:16:55.250 --> 00:16:56.130
of that number.

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:58.450
I seriously doubt most
studios will need that many,

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:00.360
but if you do, give us
a call or give Google

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:04.280
a call I'm sure we'll
very happy to talk to you.

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:06.467
What's coming next?

00:17:06.467 --> 00:17:08.800
I'd mentioned the Deadline
has asset awareness right now

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:11.060
but it doesn't really
manage assets for you.

00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:13.890
We are all over that,
we have some prototypes

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:15.310
that we showed at NAB.

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:17.810
We're building a comprehensive
system for asset management

00:17:17.810 --> 00:17:19.079
that's coming very
soon, if you want

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:20.787
to get on the beta
for that I'll tell you

00:17:20.787 --> 00:17:21.860
how to do that real soon.

00:17:21.860 --> 00:17:23.359
Simplified connectivity,
I mentioned

00:17:23.359 --> 00:17:25.630
the virtual private network
that you need to set up.

00:17:25.630 --> 00:17:27.140
A lot of studios will
probably still want

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:28.639
a virtual private
network, but we're

00:17:28.639 --> 00:17:30.490
going to come up with
a proxy server that

00:17:30.490 --> 00:17:34.290
will eliminate the need for
a VPN and still be secure,

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:39.040
as an alternative if
you want to go that way.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:42.670
Big news, we are right
on the brink of a beta

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:44.680
testing on demand licensing.

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:46.550
So the Deadline
licensing can match

00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:48.910
the kind of licensing
that you see with services

00:17:48.910 --> 00:17:50.880
like Google Cloud Platform.

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:53.179
Per minute, per hour,
monthly billing models.

00:17:53.179 --> 00:17:54.720
It works additively
so if you already

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:58.820
have permanent licenses you
can just extend beyond them

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:00.051
with the on demand licensing.

00:18:00.051 --> 00:18:02.050
We're making the system
available to third party

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:04.341
vendors so that smaller
plug-in developers and so forth

00:18:04.341 --> 00:18:08.240
can piggyback on the system
and get on demand licensing

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:08.960
as well.

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:11.168
As I said, beta testing is
starting pretty much right

00:18:11.168 --> 00:18:14.560
away, so give us a shout
if you want to try this.

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:15.540
That's pretty much it.

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:18.150
Thinkbox is building progressive
and hybridized workflows

00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:21.490
targeting end users with
no development experience.

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:23.880
But also remaining
extremely flexible

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:26.490
and configurable for
very large studios.

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:29.490
We have several very big
name, very large studios that

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:33.310
are deploying this kind
of set up right now,

00:18:33.310 --> 00:18:36.260
and you'll be hearing
more about that very soon.

00:18:36.260 --> 00:18:39.200
It's our belief that
on demand extensions

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:42.344
to existing infrastructure
is the right way to go.

00:18:42.344 --> 00:18:44.260
With that said, there's
lots of different ways

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:46.593
we've seen lots of different
solutions here, all of them

00:18:46.593 --> 00:18:48.520
are valid, all of
them provide value.

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:51.710
And so we have our
philosophy as well,

00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:56.167
and it is to extend your
existing system with on demand.

00:18:56.167 --> 00:18:56.750
And that's it.

